
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A philosopher who has &#8220;long been skeptical of journal rankings&#8221; based on polls or <a href="https://www.elsevier.com/products/scopus" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Scopus</a> data has developed a new way of assessing philosophy journals: by how frequently their articles are cited in the <a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/">Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</a> and the <a href="https://iep.utm.edu/">Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy</a>.</p>



<span id="more-27420"></span>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://www.pts.edu.pl/tadeusz-ciecierski" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Tadeusz Ciecierski</a>, a philosopher at the University of Warsaw, <a href="https://www.pts.edu.pl/tadeusz-ciecierski#journals" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">describes</a> his method:</p>



<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>The idea is simple: rank journals by citations to their articles in the bibliographies of the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy (SEP) and the Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy (IEP). The counting rule is one distinct paper per encyclopedia entry: repeated references to the same paper within one entry count once, while the same paper cited in five different entries counts five times, etc. Books and chapters in edited volumes are outside the intended scope. There are nine variants: SEP and IEP combined, SEP alone, and IEP alone, each covering papers published in 1900–2026, 1945–2026, or 1990–2026.</em></p>
</blockquote>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Ciecierski notes that this is a work in progress and that he is continuing to revise it and comb it for errors.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">He also says &#8220;Nor am I presenting this as a recommendation about where to publish. There are virtues, above all, open access, that I consider enormously important independently of any journal’s position in a ranking. Citation counts are not a complete measure of scholarly value.&#8221;</p>



